  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Snow showers abate Saturday. Colder and dry into\nthe weekend, then gradual warming.\n\nModels/satellite showing more shortwaves rounding the base of\nthe 5h trough into early Saturday before the 8h trough and low\nlevel moisture starts to push out ending any snow showers. An\ninch or two still possible into early Saturday. Winds will be\nlighter tonight but expect another surge of winds during the\nmorning Saturday behind the next wave.\n\nTemperatures across the area will be much cooler compared to\nearlier in the month, as the upper level trough remains over\nthe region through the first half of the weekend. These lower\n500mb heights combined with surface high pressure settling\ndirectly over the region by Monday night will lead to several\nnights of ideal radiational cooling with clear skies and calm\nwinds. These 500mb heights combined with 850mb temperatures in\nthe -5 to -10 degree Celsius range will keep high temperatures\n5-10 degrees below normal for the Piedmont, and 15-20 degrees\nbelow normal for locations west of the Blue Ridge. Highs each\nday through the weekend will remain in the upper 40s to low 50s\nin the Piedmont, with 30s and 40s for mountain locations.\nOvernight lows look to fall into the 20s areawide both Saturday\nand Sunday nights. \n\nBy early next week, a gradual warming trend occurs, with highs \nin the 50s by Tuesday and in the 60s by Thursday with no rain \nexpected all week.",
